o  groups of genes um in three different  comparisons  we look at tumor versus normal with  pdl-1 positive staining versus negative  staining  and we looked at microsatellite stable  versus microsatellite unstable  groups of these of these colon tumor  samples  so as i mentioned we recently came out  with a quant studio 67 pro  uh real-time system this is our flagship  uh quant  real-time pcr system and we have  introduced  several new features that make this uh  easy  easier for you to use in laboratory one  of them is that it has facial  recognition and voice commands  so you can respond to your face or  our voice commands um freeing up your  hands for doing  uh uh other things um it uh introduces  rfid uh plate readers so that on an rfid  tag um there are certain um things that  can be coded and  automatically uploaded into the  instrument i'll have more to say about  that in a second  um it's a true standalone operation with  no external computer needed  um it facilitates tech support by  having uh direct contact with tech  support  and readily downloadable files by tech  support team  to to help speed resolution of technical  problems  um and there are other things you can  read here block  ease of changing cloud connectivity  um instrument connect monitoring  all these things uh help make this  instrument  very easy to use and uh and  take your pcr experiments to the next  level  now as i mentioned the quant studio 67  pro comes with rfid  tagging um this is a radio frequency tag  that is embedded into a plate  uh where the the um the rfid  enables the smart workflow the smart  workflow  the rfid can it can include the plate  layout  all the assay information and run method  all in this rfid tag  it can be get immediately transferred to  the eds file  after the run is completed and and um  when you're running started it will be  uploaded to the instrument so you just  have to basically  put it in and and and turn it on and go  attackman assays in these rfid tag  plates are pre-spotted on the plate  and so it's very convenient then you  just add your sample and master mix and  you're off to the races  and then finally the data analysis is  very easy because you don't have to  download tacman files and upload  a plate layout once the experiment is  complete the data is immediately  analyzable because the eds file  contains all the important information  about the uh the  location of the assays and the run that  was done  so um it's the most simplified workflow  as shown here on the left-hand side of  this  this slide so  um what we did was that we used these  two publications to  to uh order rfid tagged plates  that had these two signatures embedded  in them  one of the signatures was based on an  heirs at all publication that came out  in jay  um journal of clinical investigation  2017.  um this this is a t cell and flame gene  expression signature that predicted  response to pd-1 inhibitors  again you can go back to the original  publication to find out what these genes  actually were but  this is one subset of the genes that we  that we put into the  into the into the plate  another publication came out of the  group uh shikhala at  um at al shikla shukla at all um  this was published in cell last or in  2018.  um in this in this publication they  looked at  a set of germline antigen genes that  particular resistance  that predict resistance to ctl for uh  responsiveness  um so eight different genes that that  were um were  indicative of how well the ctla4 was  going to be able to work in those cells  we also had housekeeping genes um that  were that  eight different housekeeping genes  included in the reference plate and so  we  uh ordered these rfid tagged plates that  had both the pdl1  i'm sorry the pd1 inhibitors and the  ctla inhibitors  embedded in them we then analyze those  those 10  on tumor and adjacent normal genes  colon tumor samples  and look at the differences in  expression patterns that we were able to